3.4. Related examples
In addition to the prototype BSF smart farm attempted, there have been multiple types of research done more thoroughly where IoT is implemented into the designing of smart farms, as well as BSF rearing [21,88-90]. For example, Ryu, et al. [88] has come up with a connected farm based on IoT systems, which aim to provide smart farming systems for end-users. The research used similar IoT principles and components to the prototype carried out. Mobius, an IoT service platform was used to create virtual representations of physical IoT devices [88]. The IoT service platform is compliant with the oneM2M initiative, which is an initiative started in 2012 to regulate and standardize a common machine to machine (M2M) and IoT service layer platform for an M2M service that is applicable worldwide and access-independent [89]. Besides that, a device software platform, such as a middleware known as &Cube was installed into the IoT gateways. It functions as a Java program [88]. The &Cube was installed in a Raspberry Pi, as compared to the prototype where a NodeMCU ESP8266 was used instead. As this farm was geared to agricultural purposes, more advanced sensors in addition to the temperature and humidity sensor were used, such as CO2 sensors,
